<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kholop">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kholop</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kabala people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kholop was an unfree dependant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and later Muscovy; in the 11th and 12th centuries the term covered several categories of dependent people, especially slaves. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a> set out their status: a master could kill or sell his kholop but answered for his acts. People became kholops through capture, selling themselves, debt, crime or marriage to a kholop. In 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> their status was essentially that of slaves; some served as armed retainers of landowners, and many fell into debt bondage as kabala kholops. Those who were house serfs became liable to the poll tax in 1722–1724 and were thereafter treated as ordinary serfs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kholop.jpg" alt="Kholop" width="330" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Viktor Vasnetsov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Бой_скифов_со_славянами.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kholops</b> were the unfree of Rus’: in the 11th and 12th centuries the word covered several kinds of dependent people, and especially slaves.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li></ul></section>
